# Agglomerative clustering (use AGNES, put the agglomerative coefficient somewhere visible)
# If we add the linkage methods "flexible" or "gaverage", we would have to be familiar with the Lance-Williams formula and parameters, and I do not trust my expertise in that regard. See the documentation for the `agnes()` function.
run_agnes <- function(dist) {
structure(
agnes(dist, method = "ward"),
dataset = names(object)[attributes(dist)$dataset],
algorithm = "agnes",
metric = attributes(dist)$metric,
linkage = "ward"
)
}
agnes_results <- map(metrics, run_agnes)
# What is the average agglomerative coefficient for each linkage method? This will help decide which linkage method is best for pvclust down below.
ac_values <- list(linkage = character(), ac = double(), dataset = character(), metric = character())
for (item in agnes_results) {
ac_values$linkage <- c(ac_values$linkage, attributes(item)$linkage)
ac_values$ac <- c(ac_values$ac, item$ac)
ac_values$dataset <- c(ac_values$dataset, attributes(item)$dataset)
ac_values$metric <- c(ac_values$metric, attributes(item)$metric)
}
ac_values <- as.data.frame(ac_values)
ac_values |> group_by(linkage) |> summarize(mean = mean(ac)) |> ggplot(aes(linkage, mean)) + geom_col()
# This graph shows that each linkage method is roughly equivalent, although Ward gives the largest average agglomerative coefficient by a very small margin. Since all are so similar and consistent (no value below 0.9 is reported for any data set), I feel confident using only ward for pvclust. We can remove the others.
# agnes_results %<>% discard(~ attributes(.x)$linkage != "ward")
# The worst metric for agglomerative coefficient is cosine, while the highest is Pearson's r. Euclidean distance does fairly well, with the minimum AC being 0.87 if we discount the `caesar_simplex` dataset as an outlier. Maximum is the same story, hovering around 0.9 other than `caesar_simplex`. Canberra always gets a low value when only considering Caesar. Manhattan distance has a similar problem, but it never has a lower AC than about 0.6.
